Initialize user-management domain and service layer (Spring Boot + H2)
</PREVIOUS_PR_TITLE>

<PREVIOUS_PR_DESCRIPTION>
### Motivation
- Provide the initial backend domain and service layer for a user-management module using Spring Boot and Gradle. 
- Use an in-memory H2 database for development/testing and create a minimal project scaffold so subsequent tasks can build on the domain services.

### Description
- Add Gradle build files (`build.gradle`, `settings.gradle`) and configure Java 17 toolchain and dependencies (`spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa`, `h2`).
- Implement Spring Boot entrypoint `UserManagementApplication` and JPA `User` entity with `id`, `username`, `email`, `password`, `createdAt`, plus profile/password update methods.
- Add `UserRepository` (lookup and uniqueness methods), `UserService` interface, and `UserServiceImpl` with transactional CRUD operations and uniqueness validation.
- Configure H2 and JPA in `src/main/resources/application.properties`, add a baseline context-load test `UserManagementApplicationTests`, create `.gitignore`, and persist the received prompts into `user-prompt.log` at repository root.
- Used `skill-creator` to guide the project scaffolding and file layout decisions.

### Testing
- Ran `gradle test`, which failed due to blocked dependency/plugin resolution caused by outbound access restrictions to the Gradle Plugin Portal/Maven (HTTP 403 CONNECT tunnel). 
- Verified `java -version` and `gradle -v` locally with `JAVA_HOME` set to a Java 17 toolchain and both commands succeeded. 
- Performed `curl -I` checks against the Gradle plugin repo and Maven Central which returned HTTP 403 due to the environment network/proxy restrictions, preventing dependency resolution and test execution.
